66
00:05:29,906 --> 00:05:35,832
within its data flow, streaming
systems have to rely on additional

67
00:05:35,870 --> 00:05:44,939
mechanisms to ensure correctness
And for that, we use anti-entropy.

68
00:05:45,328 --> 00:05:50,070
Anti-entropy is a distributed systems
technique that periodically compares

69
00:05:50,075 --> 00:05:53,899
data across nodes, specifically
a producer and a consumer.

70
00:05:54,415 --> 00:06:00,193
If data is being sent in a
synchronous, fashion, then to make

71
00:06:00,194 --> 00:06:04,668
sure that the consumer got the
data that the producer produced, we

72
00:06:04,846 --> 00:06:06,630
generally follow these four steps.

73
00:06:07,189 --> 00:06:11,490
We compare the data sets, we check
the data sets across the producers

74
00:06:11,490 --> 00:06:15,014
and the consumers to make sure
that each of them receive the data.

75
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:21,513
and if we see something is missing,
this next step is detection, and in

76
00:06:21,514 --> 00:06:26,985
that we see if something is missing or
corrupted, then we try to repair it.

77
00:06:27,589 --> 00:06:31,842
And once we repair it, we verify that,
okay, indeed, that the data matches

78
00:06:31,845 --> 00:06:33,565
between the producers and consumers.

79
00:06:34,792 --> 00:06:38,664
Within security, a vulnerability
finding that silently disappears

80
00:06:38,665 --> 00:06:40,809
can be dangerous as a system outage.

81
00:06:41,419 --> 00:06:45,184
So anti-entropy is the safety
net in such an environment.

82
00:06:46,184 --> 00:06:50,003
So here's the architecture at a
very high level that we follow.

83
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:54,621
so it's, it's kind of two parts
in parallel within the platform.

84
00:06:55,081 --> 00:06:59,840
this kind of design allows us
to optimize for speed without

85
00:06:59,852 --> 00:07:01,427
sacrificing on correctness.

86
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:07,791
So as you can see, all the information
about the vulnerabilities is flowing

87
00:07:07,795 --> 00:07:13,880
through as events, and then we apply
business logic on that at scale

88
00:07:14,377 --> 00:07:18,765
and speed during the enrichment
process, and make sure that the data

89
00:07:18,785 --> 00:07:20,719
reaches its respective destination.

90
00:07:21,378 --> 00:07:24,869
Now, within these four steps, there
are multiple distributed systems,

91
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:26,971
that are managed by different teams.

92
00:07:27,282 --> 00:07:33,619
And what-- That's where the second part
comes in, which is the anti-entropy.

93
00:07:34,093 --> 00:07:41,852
Between each producer and consumer, we
have a pipe or a event stream that is

94
00:07:41,858 --> 00:07:44,375
funneled to the reconciliation system.

95
00:07:44,965 --> 00:07:50,724
The reconciliation system makes sure that,
it compares what the consumer is receiving

96
00:07:50,755 --> 00:07:56,649
from every producer and what the producer
is sending, and it does this difference

97
00:07:56,651 --> 00:08:02,331
detection at scale, and wherever it finds
a difference, a repair is also initiated

98
00:08:03,331 --> 00:08:10,835
So naturally, in this kind of, environment
where data is flowing at such high,

99
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:17,696
high scale and volume, performance
and cost becomes a constraint.

100
00:08:18,663 --> 00:08:21,339
So, we, we do-- we deal with that.

101
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:28,108
One of the primary ways we deal with that
is to minimize the computation that we

102
00:08:28,124 --> 00:08:34,418
need to perform to be able to compare and
just make sure that there's no data loss.

103
00:08:34,924 --> 00:08:38,544
And for that, we, primarily
rely on Merkle trees.

104
00:08:39,603 --> 00:08:43,513
So when we are collecting-- when the
reconciliation system is collecting

105
00:08:43,515 --> 00:08:48,115
data, we partition the data in
a specific kind of structure.

106
00:08:48,869 --> 00:08:54,643
And while we are partitioning, we
also compute hashes, and that makes

107
00:08:54,657 --> 00:08:56,354
the comparison really efficient.

108
00:08:56,726 --> 00:09:02,790
So all we try to do is the first
level of defense is we compare

109
00:09:03,086 --> 00:09:07,525
the hashes and across the producer
and consumer, and if those hashes

110
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:10,238
match, then we know that the data…

111
00:09:10,314 --> 00:09:12,990
Like, we don't need to compare
the underlying data, but if

112
00:09:12,990 --> 00:09:15,894
there's a mismatch, then we
go deeper and try to compare.

113
00:09:16,855 --> 00:09:21,793
And this, this, reduces the cost
of computation while performing

114
00:09:21,794 --> 00:09:28,119
anti-entropy for a large scale data
system so the hashing itself is done

115
00:09:28,153 --> 00:09:34,411
at a attribute level, because, because
a record can exist in both systems

116
00:09:34,438 --> 00:09:36,269
while containing wrong information.

117
00:09:36,926 --> 00:09:41,180
So existing checks-- existence
checks alone are not enough.

118
00:09:41,536 --> 00:09:47,057
As you can see in this example, system
A could have delivered a record about

119
00:09:47,059 --> 00:09:50,620
a vulnerability where the severity
is critical, and system B, let's say,

120
00:09:50,653 --> 00:09:55,976
received it or not received it, but
has a record with medium as a severity.

121
00:09:56,613 --> 00:10:01,220
If we just match the IDs of the record,
that would, that would be insufficient.

122
00:10:01,751 --> 00:10:09,283
And hence we, we compute a hash for--
hash of the object at an attribute level.

123
00:10:09,454 --> 00:10:14,475
This lets us account for missing or
null fields, any kind of modified

124
00:10:14,475 --> 00:10:18,283
field, like ownership or any context.

125
00:10:18,878 --> 00:10:24,508
So the reconciliation layer detects not
only missing findings but also silent

126
00:10:24,508 --> 00:10:32,256
corruption So once, once we detect, like,
discrepancies, then comes the repair.

127
00:10:32,775 --> 00:10:38,365
And for repair, we have two techniques
that we apply or, like, two situation--

128
00:10:38,574 --> 00:10:40,031
yeah, two techniques that we apply.

129
00:10:40,299 --> 00:10:44,527
So the two techniques are read,
repair and hinted handoff.

130
00:10:45,090 --> 00:10:51,700
So when-- whenever we see an inconsistent
version of the data, we simply re-drive

131
00:10:51,902 --> 00:10:58,165
the, the data that from the producer,
to the consumer, which we have detected

132
00:10:58,166 --> 00:11:04,534
is incorrect, and that, that restores
the consumer to the correct state.

133
00:11:05,677 --> 00:11:09,824
And sometimes when, like, let's
say a consumer is not available,

134
00:11:10,066 --> 00:11:16,068
and that also leads to, situations
where the anti-entropy sy-system

135
00:11:16,071 --> 00:11:18,366
detects that data might be missing.

136
00:11:19,158 --> 00:11:23,983
And in those situations, we have
created operations where, the

137
00:11:24,005 --> 00:11:28,310
reconciler would wait until the
consumer system recovers, and once it

138
00:11:28,310 --> 00:11:33,312
recovers, it re-drives the data that,
it needs to repair the situation.

139
00:11:34,122 --> 00:11:38,642
So together, these mechanisms ensure
that infrastructure blips don't become

140
00:11:38,648 --> 00:11:44,802
permanent gaps in your security data So
all in all together, it looks as follows.

141
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:48,760
Like, it combines real-time
processing with, like, the side

142
00:11:49,106 --> 00:11:51,507
periodic reconciliation that runs.

143
00:11:52,112 --> 00:11:55,756
So we ingest information, we enrich
it, and then we streaming it--

144
00:11:55,800 --> 00:11:57,935
stream it out to our consumers.

145
00:11:58,321 --> 00:12:02,558
And side by side, we are, like,
reconciling and making sure that

146
00:12:02,573 --> 00:12:05,659
the data that is being vended out
is correct, and we are able to

147
00:12:05,664 --> 00:12:07,678
repair it as soon as possible.

148
00:12:08,901 --> 00:12:14,482
So the result is fast delivery
under normal conditions with bounded

149
00:12:14,510 --> 00:12:19,653
auditable recovery when failures occur
lastly, auditability and compliance.

150
00:12:19,681 --> 00:12:25,062
Like in our regulated environments,
the, like AWS, the repair

151
00:12:25,063 --> 00:12:26,351
itself must be auditable.

152
00:12:26,527 --> 00:12:31,220
Automated corrections without records
create their own compliance risk.

153
00:12:31,653 --> 00:12:39,654
So every, every reconciliation action is
itself captured, and repairing security

154
00:12:39,655 --> 00:12:43,849
data without recording the repair creates
another compliance risk by itself.

155
00:12:44,793 --> 00:12:50,138
So, w- we-- the finding identifier
is we, we make sure that

156
00:12:50,149 --> 00:12:54,822
the finding re-identifier is
re-restored to its original state,

157
00:12:55,427 --> 00:12:57,471
if we detected inconsistency.

158
00:12:58,017 --> 00:13:03,224
and then we also capture like where,
what the source of truth for that was.

159
00:13:03,567 --> 00:13:09,335
We capture every repair action that was
taken, and naturally that has like it's…

160
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:15,588
We store like exactly when these events
occurred, with, with their verification

161
00:13:15,589 --> 00:13:23,020
result So largely the pattern for
anti-entropy, in our cloud scale

162
00:13:23,020 --> 00:13:24,926
security platform looks as follows.

163
00:13:25,610 --> 00:13:28,261
Like to make sure that…

164
00:13:29,044 --> 00:13:34,731
So the bottommost layer is the data source
layer, where we are sourcing the data from

165
00:13:34,745 --> 00:13:40,991
multiple scanners, multiple other security
